<p>Property registration in India makes the transfer of ownership legally binding — an unregistered sale deed is not valid and does not confer legal title under the Registration Act 1908. The total cost of registering a property has four components. First, <strong>stamp duty</strong>: a state government tax (rates vary 3–8% by state — there is no national uniform rate; see the Stamp Duty Calculator for state-specific rates and verify with your state's registration authority). Second, <strong>registration fee</strong>: typically 1% of the property's registered value, paid to the sub-registrar's office for recording the deed — some states cap this at ₹30,000–50,000 for high-value properties (verify at your state's registration website). Third, <strong>legal and documentation charges</strong>: advocate fees for drafting the sale deed and verifying title (typically ₹10,000–50,000 depending on property value and city), stamp paper/e-stamp charges, and documentation. Fourth, for <strong>under-construction properties</strong>: GST at 1% for affordable housing (units priced below ₹45 lakh) or 5% for regular new construction — no GST on resale properties (verify current thresholds at cbic.gov.in). Example for a ₹1 crore property in Maharashtra: stamp duty ₹6L (6% male buyer) + registration ₹1L (1%) + legal ₹25,000 = approximately ₹7.25 lakh in government and legal charges, all payable from personal funds at registration (not financeable through the home loan).</p>

<p>The calculator above uses an assumed stamp duty rate (6% is used as a placeholder for illustration purposes — this is not a national rate). For an accurate calculation, you must enter your state's actual stamp duty rate, which depends on your state, buyer gender, property type, and in some states the property value slab. The registration is done at the sub-registrar's office (SRO) in the jurisdiction where the property is located. Both buyer and seller must be present in person with original documents, or a Power of Attorney holder can represent them. Documents required at registration: original sale agreement, identity proofs, PAN cards of both parties, and Form 26QB if the property value exceeds ₹50 lakh (TDS at 1% on the consideration, deductible by the buyer and deposited on behalf of the seller). After registration, the buyer's name is mutated in the local municipal records (property card / 7/12 extract / patta) — this mutation step, required for future tax payments and resale, has its own municipal charge (typically ₹500–5,000 depending on the municipality). Section 80C allows a deduction for stamp duty and registration charges paid on a new residential property in the year of payment under the old regime (up to the combined ₹1.5L cap). Use the <a href="/india/stamp-duty-calculator/">Stamp Duty Calculator</a> for the state-specific stamp duty rate and the <a href="/india/home-loan-emi-calculator/">Home Loan EMI Calculator</a> for the ongoing monthly cost after registration.</p>
